(Folklore, legends, myths and fairy tales have followed childhood) Tj
T*
(through the ages, for every healthy youngster has a wholesome and) Tj
T*
(instinctive love for stories fantastic, marvelous and manifestly) Tj
T*
(unreal. The winged fairies of Grimm and Andersen have brought more) Tj
T*
(happiness to childish hearts than all other human creations.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Yet the old time fairy tale, having served for generations, may now be) Tj
T*
(classed as "historical" in the children's library; for the time has) Tj
T*
(come for a series of newer "wonder tales" in which the stereotyped) Tj
T*
(genie, dwarf and fairy are eliminated, together with all the horrible) Tj
T*
(and blood-curdling incidents devised by their authors to point a) Tj
T*
(fearsome moral to each tale. Modern education includes morality;) Tj
T*
(therefore the modern child seeks only entertainment in its wonder tales) Tj
T*
(and gladly dispenses with all disagreeable incident.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Having this thought in mind, the story of "The Wonderful Wizard of Oz") Tj
T*
(was written solely to please children of today. It aspires to being a) Tj
T*
(modernized fairy tale, in which the wonderment and joy are retained and) Tj
T*
(the heartaches and nightmares are left out.) Tj
